{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/902035-recs-for-the-dark-eye","id":902035,"title":"Recs for \"The Dark Eye\"?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Additional Discussion","Off-Topic"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-05-28T07:23:30-07:00","id":902035,"title":"Recs for \"The Dark Eye\"?","content":"Can anyone recommend <b>The Dark Eye </b>(<i>Das Schwarze Auge</i>)? Its a German fantasy RPG that reminds me a lot of D&amp;D, and they're kickstarting an English version of Fifth Edition. Any thoughts? Cool ideas, major flaws, weird idiosyncracies?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-05-28T16:11:07-07:00","id":902342,"title":"","content":"I've only played videogames based on the TTRPG - Chains of Satinav, Memoria, and Blackguards. From those, I glean that TDE has a pretty solid Dark Fantasy setting. It might feel kind of bleak depending on how you choose to play - it never gets as bad as, say, the Warhammer 'verse, but I see a general trend of inhumanity through the characters and locations, and a lot of stories that end with decidedly pyrrhic victories.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":908,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","user":"semicasual","date":". 05-28-2016, 07:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T11:00:18-07:00","id":905056,"title":"","content":"Hmm . . . Anyone familiar with the pen-and-paper version? I'm already backing the <b>Ether</b> and <b>Faith: a Garden in Hell</b> Kickstarters, so I'll have plenty of new stuff to keep me busy for a while. But if its something really great, I don't want to miss out, either . . .<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>semicasual</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/off-topic/902035-recs-for-the-dark-eye?p=902342#post902342\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I've only played videogames based on the TTRPG - Chains of Satinav, Memoria, and Blackguards. From those, I glean that TDE has a pretty solid Dark Fantasy setting. It might feel kind of bleak depending on how you choose to play - it never gets as bad as, say, the Warhammer 'verse, but I see a general trend of inhumanity through the characters and locations, and a lot of stories that end with decidedly pyrrhic victories.</div> </div> </div> </div>Speaking of the video games - the title <i>Realms of Arkania</i> sounds very familiar to me. Did one of them give you a choice between four player characters, one a cat-person named Kieran?<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4624-one-vorlon","name":"One Vorlon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"537"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-01T15:50:39-07:00","id":905231,"title":"","content":"I play TDE since 25 years and 4 editions and the game still hasn't driven me away, so I guess it is not totally bad :P<br /> <br /> Joke aside, TDE might not be the second coming of Ingenuity, but it's a very solid game. It has working rules, a very detailed background and an ongoing not-really-metaplot-but-more-like-living-history of Ethra (the world it is set in) that has grown since almost 3 decades. The books belong to the better written RPGs out there and information is very dense and over the last 4 editions much background was described.<br /> <br /> It has a handful of quirky rules which are unique (non-combat skill-roll: each skill is linked to three attributes; make three d20-roll-equal/under-rolls, one for each attribute; if you roll too high, spend skillpoints to lower the die-result; if you have 0+ skillpoints left after the third roll, you succeed, if you cannot pay down all dice, you fail) and might seem cumbersome to new players, but they are easy to learn and honestly also easy to use. Hmm ... maybe watch <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mk1hiPp2nlI\" target=\"_blank\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mk1hiPp2nlI</a>. Mh\u00e1ire Stritter is a TDE author and sorta the public spokesperson for the franchise atm <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> The great strength of TDE is in my opinion the detailed, dense background. It has something that's difficult to actually pinpoint that makes it feel more real than many other RPGs. I'm not sure if I can adequately describe it, but despite TDE being played on fantasy!medieval!themepark-continent with vikings and high medieval not-german empire and not-italian late renaissance and not-orient with not-1001-nights and a dozen other inspirations, somehow the world feels more realistic than many other RPGs. My guess is that's because it was written by europeans and perhaps subconsciously (maybe sub-culturally?) we see and experience &quot;medieval&quot; differently than american authors which where raised in a culture that was younger than these times. Honestly, I don't even know if this is actually a selling point, I don't know if you (assuming that you are american) could tell the difference or if it would even matter to you, but ... well ... it's is a very big part of the feeling or immersion that makes me love the game, so I guess I should have mentioned it.<br /> <br /> ^^;<br /> <br /> Anyway, what semicasual says about the pc games, is in my opinion not representative for the whole gameline. The bleak mood fits in the world, yes, but the games are also set in very bleak places during very dark times on the continent. Realms of Arkania was set during wartime and wasn't that bleak (especially not part 3) and I think the games Drakensang and River of Time are better representations of the TTRPG.<br /> <br /> Hm ... not sure what else I could tell you. Maybe you could ask aspects that interest you and I answer.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/643-taliesin","name":"Taliesin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"27"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}